body:
## Summary

`Entity.getEntityOrNull` catches only `EntityNotFoundException`. Asking it for a field the entity's
schema does not declare raises `IllegalArgumentException` from `EntityUtil.Fields`, which escapes the
alert matcher and aborts the whole change-event batch. That is the failure mode #28304 and #29674
were meant to close, reached through a different exception.

Concretely: an alert on resource `all` with `filterByDomain` stops processing its batch when an
ordinary `entityUpdated` arrives for a `domain`, because `domain.json` has no `domains` property.

## Root cause

`allowedFields` is exactly the set of properties the entity's JSON Schema declares:

```java
// EntityRepository.java:664
allowedFields = getEntityFields(entityClass);

// Entity.java:903
public static <T> Set<String> getEntityFields(Class<T> clz) {
  JsonPropertyOrder propertyOrder = clz.getAnnotation(JsonPropertyOrder.class);
  return new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(propertyOrder.value()));
}
```

Asking for a field outside that set throws:

```java
// EntityUtil.java:523
public Fields(Set<String> allowedFields, String fieldsParam) {
  ...
  for (String field : fieldList) {
    if (!allowedFields.contains(field)) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException(CatalogExceptionMessage.invalidField(field));
    }
  }
}
```

Both `getEntityOrNull` overloads narrow their catch to `EntityNotFoundException`, and
`entityRepository.getFields(fields)` sits inside the guarded block, so the
`IllegalArgumentException` propagates:

```java
// Entity.java:641
public static <T> T getEntityOrNull(EntityReference entityReference, String field, Include include) {
  if (entityReference == null) return null;
  try {
    return Entity.getEntity(entityReference, field, include);
  } catch (EntityNotFoundException e) {
    return null;
  }
}

// Entity.java:667
public static <T> T getEntityOrNull(String entityType, UUID id, String fields, RelationIncludes ri) {
  EntityRepository<?> entityRepository = Entity.getEntityRepository(entityType);
  try {
    entity = (T) entityRepository.get(null, id, entityRepository.getFields(fields), ri, true);
  } catch (EntityNotFoundException e) {
    ...
  }
}
```

An unknown *entity type* is handled, because `Entity.getEntityRepository` (`Entity.java:759`) throws
`EntityNotFoundException`. A known type with an undeclared *field* is not.

## Entity types that trigger it

`EntityInterface` declares `getOwners()` and `getDomains()` as defaults returning `null`, so entities
whose schema omits those properties compile and read as "no owners / no domains", which is exactly
what sends the matchers to the store for a second look.

| Schema | `owners` | `domains` |
|---|---|---|
| `entity/teams/user.json` | absent | present |
| `entity/domains/domain.json` | present | absent |
| `entity/data/dataContract.json` | present | absent |

## Reachable today, without threads

`AlertsRuleEvaluator.matchAnyDomain` always re-reads the stored entity:

```java
// AlertsRuleEvaluator.java:505
private boolean matchesEntityOrTestSuiteDomain(EntityInterface entity, List<String> domainFqns) {
  EntityInterface storedEntity = readStoredEntity(entity.getId(), Entity.FIELD_DOMAINS);
  ...
}

// AlertsRuleEvaluator.java:536
private <T extends EntityInterface> T readStoredEntity(UUID entityId, String fields) {
  return Entity.getEntityOrNull(changeEvent.getEntityType(), entityId, fields, DELETED_TOLERANT_SUBJECT);
}
```

So: an alert on resource `all` with `filterByDomain`, plus any `entityUpdated` on a `domain`, throws
`IllegalArgumentException: Invalid field domains` out of the matcher.

`matchAnyOwnerName` reaches it on the same alert whenever the payload carries no owners
(`AlertsRuleEvaluator.java:150-155`), for instance an `entityUpdated` on a `user`, since `user.json`
declares no `owners`.

No thread event is required for either. #30571 adds two further call sites on the thread path
(`threadSubjectMatchesOwner` / `threadSubjectMatchesDomain`), which widen who hits this but do not
introduce it.

## Suggested fix

Make `getEntityOrNull` honour its contract: a field the entity cannot supply should yield "no value",
not an exception. Either use `getOnlySupportedFields` on these best-effort paths, or widen the catch
to include `IllegalArgumentException` and log at debug.

Filed separately from #30555 / #30571 because `getEntityOrNull` and `readStoredEntity` are shared by
every alert evaluation path and by callers well outside alerting, so the blast radius of the change
is different from the thread-scoping fix.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] An alert on `all` with `filterByDomain` processes an `entityUpdated` on a `domain` without
      throwing, and the filter evaluates to `false`.
- [ ] The same for `filterByOwnerName` and an `entityUpdated` on a `user`.
- [ ] A single event that cannot supply the requested field does not abort the remaining events in
      the batch.
- [ ] Unit coverage for a requested field absent from the entity's `allowedFields`.

