**Affected module**
Ingestion

**Describe the bug**

When parsing more complex avro schema, where we have object 'Item' (from example below) which can have property 'itemList', which is additional list of 'Item' objects, this will cause infinite recursion issue and failure of whole ingestion.

Ideally such schema should be parsed properly however upon RecursionException it would be acceptable to skip it. It is not the case and whole Python process exists with SIGABORT signal - the suspected culprit here is C library used for avro parsing.

**To Reproduce**

```
from metadata.parsers.avro_parser import parse_avro_schema


data = """
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "RecursionIssue",
  "namespace": "com.issue.recursion",
  "doc": "Schema with recursion issue",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "issue",
      "type": {
        "type": "record",
        "name": "Issue",
        "doc": "Global Schema Name",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "itemList",
            "default": null,
            "type": [
              "null",
              {
                "type": "array",
                "items": {
                  "type": "record",
                  "name": "Item",
                  "doc": "Item List  - Array of Sub Schema",
                  "fields": [
                    {
                      "name": "itemList",
                      "type": [
                        "null",
                        {
                          "type": "array",
                          "items": "Item"
                        }
                      ],
                      "default": null
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}

"""
try:
    print(parse_avro_schema(data))
except RecursionError as e:
    print(e.args)
```

**Expected behavior**
Schema is parsed properly (or Python code doesn't fail with SIGABRT).

**Version:**
 - OS: [e.g. iOS]
 - Python version: 3.9
 - OpenMetadata version: [e.g. 0.8] 1.3.1
 - OpenMetadata Ingestion package version: [e.g. `openmetadata-ingestion[docker]==XYZ`] 1.3.1

**Additional context**
This is different from https://github.com/open-metadata/OpenMetadata/issues/13371

**Context**
Metadata ingestion performs a PATCH request when updating information about entities. When patching arrays (e.g. columns for table entity) if the order of the array is different from the existing array for the entity, we'll be patching the wrong element.

**Example**
Let's say my table column names are ordered as follows 
```
['jurisdiction name', 'count participants', 'count female', 'percent female', 'count male', 'percent male', 'partition_0']
```
If the next time I am processing metadata the order of the column I retrieve from the system change let's say as shown below[...]
```
['partition_0', 'jurisdiction name', 'count participants', 'count female', 'percent female', 'count male', 'percent male',]
```
[...] my patch request will look like this
```
[{'op': 'add', 'path': '/tablePartition', 'value': {'columns': [...], 'intervalType': 'COLUMN-VALUE'}}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/tableType', 'value': 'Partitioned'}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/columns/0/name', 'value': 'partition_0'}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/columns/0/dataTypeDisplay', 'value': 'string'}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/columns/0/dataType', 'value': 'STRING'}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/columns/1/name', 'value': 'jurisdiction name'}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/columns/2/name', 'value': 'count participants'}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/columns/3/name', 'value': 'count female'}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/columns/3/dataTypeDisplay', 'value': 'bigint'}
{'op': 'replace', 'path': '/columns/3/dataType', 'value': 'BIGINT'}]
```
Where I will be patching column name at index 0 with value `partition_0` effectively changing the name of the column resulting in a mis match description.

**How to reproduce it**
Spin up OM 1.2.5 with an Athena service (ingest `om_test_glue`). Add description to columns. Upgrade to 1.3.1 (we added support for table partition in athena in 1.3.1 and the partitioned column is at index 0 in the list of columns). You will now see shifted description -- see screenshots.

![Screenshot 2024-03-14 at 10 13 09 AM](https://github.com/open-metadata/OpenMetadata/assets/13626425/f2d36123-aad8-4261-9fe5-caaef98d9ff7)
![Screenshot 2024-03-14 at 9 26 46 AM](https://github.com/open-metadata/OpenMetadata/assets/13626425/5dc77cc9-dee7-4d0e-8e5c-1e8b53014659)

